        "label": "backtracking",
        "text": "But wait, hold on. The definition is (a, b) ∈ R if and only if a + b is divisible by 3. So, for reflexivity, we need (a, a) ∈ R for all a in S."
      },
      {
        "label": "deduction",
        "text": "Since for a = 1, 2, 4, 5, (a, a) is not in R, so reflexivity fails. So, R is not reflexive."
      },
      {
        "label": "initializing",
        "text": "**Checking Symmetry:**"
      },
      {
        "label": "deduction",
        "text": "Symmetry requires that if (a, b) ∈ R, then (b, a) ∈ R. Since a + b is divisible by 3, then b + a is also divisible by 3 because addition is commutative. So, if a + b is divisible by 3, then b + a is also divisible by 3. Therefore, symmetry holds."
      },
      {
        "label": "initializing",
        "text": "**Checking Transitivity:**\n\nThis is the tricky part. I need to check if for all a, b, c in S, if (a, b) ∈ R and (b, c) ∈ R, then (a, c) ∈ R. So, if a + b is divisible by 3 and b + c is divisible by 3, then a + c must be divisible by 3."
