      {
        "label": "initializing",
        "text": "Okay, so I need to prove that the logical statement ((P → Q) ∧ (Q → R) ∧ ¬R) → ¬P is a tautology."
      },
      {
        "label": "adding-knowledge",
        "text": "Hmm, a tautology means that it's always true, regardless of the truth values of P, Q, and R. I remember that tautologies can often be proven using natural deduction or by showing that the statement is logically equivalent to something that's obviously true."
      },
      {
        "label": "adding-knowledge",
        "text": "First, let me recall what each part of the statement means. P → Q is \"if P then Q,\" Q → R is \"if Q then R,\" and ¬R is \"not R.\""
      },
      {
        "label": "initializing",
        "text": "So the entire antecedent is \"if P then Q, and if Q then R, and not R.\" The conclusion is \"not P.\" So, if all those conditions are met, then P must be false."
      },
      {
        "label": "initializing",
        "text": "I think I can approach this using natural deduction. Maybe start by assuming the antecedent is true and then derive ¬P. Let me try that."
      },
      {
        "label": "deduction",
        "text": "1. Assume (P → Q) ∧ (Q → R) ∧ ¬R. Let's break this down into three separate assumptions: P → Q, Q → R, and ¬R."
      },
      {
        "label": "uncertainty-estimation",
        "text": "2. From ¬R, I can use the Law of Excluded Middle to consider R and ¬R. Wait, but since we have ¬R, maybe I can use that directly."
