      {
        "label": "deduction",
        "text": "3. Now, since we have Q → R and ¬R, can I derive ¬Q? Because if Q were true, then R would have to be true, which contradicts ¬R. So, by modus tollens, from Q → R and ¬R, we can conclude ¬Q."
      },
      {
        "label": "deduction",
        "text": "4. Now, we have ¬Q. If we have ¬Q and P → Q, can we derive ¬P? Yes, because if P were true, then Q would have to be true, which contradicts ¬Q. So, by modus tollens again, from P → Q and ¬Q, we can conclude ¬P."
      },
      {
        "label": "deduction",
        "text": "5. So, from the antecedent, we've derived ¬P. Therefore, the entire implication ((P → Q) ∧ (Q → R) ∧ ¬R) → ¬P is a tautology."
      },
