    "chain_full": "Okay, so I need to prove that (A ∪ B) - C is equal to (A - C) ∪ (B - C). Hmm, set operations can be a bit tricky, but I think I can handle this by using the definitions provided. Let me recall that for any set X and Y, x ∈ (X - Y) if and only if x is in X and x is not in Y. So, I need to show that every element in (A ∪ B) - C is also in (A - C) ∪ (B - C), and vice versa. That should establish the equality.\n\nFirst, let me write down what each side means using the definition. \n\nStarting with the left-hand side: (A ∪ B) - C. By definition, an element x is in (A ∪ B) - C if and only if x is in (A ∪ B) and x is not in C. \n\nNow, (A ∪ B) is the set of all elements that are in A or in B or in both. So, x ∈ (A ∪ B) means x is in A or x is in B. Therefore, (A ∪ B) - C is the set of all elements that are in A or B, but not in C.\n\nOn the other hand, the right-hand side is (A - C) ∪ (B - C). Let's break that down. (A - C) is the set of elements in A that are not in C, and (B - C) is the set of elements in B that are not in C. So, their union is all elements that are either in A but not in C, or in B but not in C, or in both.\n\nSo, to show that (A ∪ B) - C equals (A - C) ∪ (B - C), I need to show that each side is a subset of the other.\n\nLet me start by showing that (A ∪ B) - C is a subset of (A - C) ∪ (B - C). \n\nTake any element x in (A ∪ B) - C. By definition, x is in (A ∪ B) and x is not in C. Since x is in (A ∪ B), it must be in A or in B. So, there are two cases:\n\n1. x is in A but not in C. In this case, x is in (A - C), so it's in the union (A - C) ∪ (B - C).\n\n2. x is in B but not in C. Similarly, x is in (B - C), so it's also in the union.\n\nTherefore, in either case, x is in (A - C) ∪ (B - C). So, every element of (A ∪ B) - C is in (A - C) ∪ (B - C). That proves that (A ∪ B) - C is a subset of (A - C) ∪ (B - C).\n\nNow, I need to show the reverse: that (A - C) ∪ (B - C) is a subset of (A ∪ B) - C. Take any element x in (A - C) ∪ (B - C). That means x is either in (A - C) or in (B - C), or in both.\n\nCase 1: x is in (A - C). Then, x is in A and x is not in C. Since x is in A, it's certainly in (A ∪ B). Also, since x is not in C, x is in (A ∪ B) - C. So, x is in (A ∪ B) - C.\n\nCase 2: x is in (B - C). Similarly, x is in B and x is not in C. So, x is in (A ∪ B) because it's in B, and x is not in C, so x is in (A ∪ B) - C.\n\nCase 3: x is in both (A - C) and (B - C). Then, x is in A and not in C, and also in B and not in C. So, x is in A ∪ B, and since it's not in C, it's in (A ∪ B) - C.\n\nIn all cases, x is in (A ∪ B) - C. Therefore, every element of (A - C) ∪ (B - C) is in (A ∪ B) - C. So, (A - C) ∪ (B - C) is a subset of (A ∪ B) - C.\n\nSince we've shown both subsets, we can conclude that (A ∪ B) - C equals (A - C) ∪ (B - C). That seems to make sense. Let me just double-check if I missed anything.\n\nWait, is there any case where an element could be in both (A ∪ B) - C and not in (A - C) ∪ (B - C)? I don't think so because we've covered all possibilities. If x is in (A ∪ B) - C, it's in A or B but not in C, so it must be in either (A - C) or (B - C). Conversely, if x is in (A - C) or (B - C), it's in A or B but not in C, so it's in (A ∪ B) - C. Yeah, that seems solid.\n\nI think that's a thorough proof. I used the definitions, considered all cases, and showed mutual inclusion. I don't see any gaps or mistakes in the logic. So, I'm confident that (A ∪ B) - C is indeed equal to (A - C) ∪ (B - C).\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe equality is proven, so \\boxed{(A \\cup B) - C = (A - C) \\cup (B - C)}.\n</think>\n\nTo prove that \\((A \\cup B) - C = (A - C) \\cup (B - C)\\), we will show mutual subset inclusion.\n\nFirst, we show that \\((A \\cup B) - C \\subseteq (A - C) \\cup (B - C)\\).\n\nTake any element \\(x \\in (A \\cup B) - C\\). By definition, \\(x\\) is in \\(A \\cup B\\) and \\(x\\) is not in \\(C\\). Since \\(x\\) is in \\(A \\cup B\\), it must be in \\(A\\) or \\(B\\). Therefore, there are two cases:\n1. If \\(x \\in A\\) and \\(x \\notin C\\), then \\(x \\in (A - C)\\).\n2. If \\(x \\in B\\) and \\(x \\notin C\\), then \\(x \\in (B - C)\\).\n\nIn either case, \\(x\\) is in \\((A - C) \\cup (B - C)\\). Thus, every element of \\((A \\cup B) - C\\) is in \\((A - C) \\cup (B - C)\\), proving that \\((A \\cup B) - C \\subseteq (A - C) \\cup (B - C)\\).\n\nNext, we show that \\((A - C) \\cup (B - C) \\subseteq (A \\cup B) - C\\).\n\nTake any element \\(x \\in (A - C) \\cup (B - C)\\). This means \\(x\\) is either in \\(A - C\\) or in \\(B - C\\), or in both.\n1. If \\(x \\in (A - C)\\), then \\(x \\in A\\) and \\(x \\notin C\\). Hence, \\(x \\in A \\cup B\\) and \\(x \\notin C\\), so \\(x \\in (A \\cup B) - C\\).\n2. If \\(x \\in (B - C)\\), then \\(x \\in B\\) and \\(x \\notin C\\). Hence, \\(x \\in A \\cup B\\) and \\(x \\notin C\\), so \\(x \\in (A \\cup B) - C\\).\n3. If \\(x \\in (A - C)\\) and \\(x \\in (B - C)\\), then \\(x \\in A\\) and \\(x \\notin C\\), so \\(x \\in (A \\cup B) - C\\).\n\nIn all cases, \\(x\\) is in \\((A \\cup B) - C\\). Thus, every element of \\((A - C) \\cup (B - C)\\) is in \\((A \\cup B) - C\\), proving that \\((A - C) \\cup (B - C) \\subseteq (A \\cup B) - C\\).\n\nSince both subsets are shown, we conclude that \\((A \\cup B) - C = (A - C) \\cup (B - C)\\).\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{(A \\cup B) - C = (A - C) \\cup (B - C)}\n\\]",
