xis from z=0 to z=6.\n\nWait, no, actually, the columns are at specific (x, y) positions, but their height is from 0 to 6. So, each column is a vertical cylinder along the z-axis from z=0 to z=6, centered at (x_i, y_i). So, the equation of each column is (x - x_i)^2 + (y - y_i)^2 = 0.25, 0 ≤ z ≤ 6.\n\nSo, to find if the drone's path intersects any of these columns, we can compute the distance from the line (drone's path) to each column's center (x_i, y_i). If this distance is less than or equal to 0.5, then the line intersects the cylinder.\n\nSo, let's compute the distance from the line AB to each column's center (x_i, y_i). If this distance is ≤ 0.5, then the line intersects the cylinder.\n\nSo, let's compute this distance for each column.\n\nFirst, let's compute the distance from point (x_i, y_i, z_i) to the line AB.\n\nWait, but the point is (x_i, y_i, z_i), but the line AB is in 3D space. So, the distance from the point to the line is given by the formula:\n\ndistance = |(B - A) × (P - A)| / |B - A|\n\nWhere P is the point (x_i, y_i, z_i).\n\nSo, let's compute this for each column.\n\nFirst, let's compute vector AB: (36, 26, -4)\n\nThen, for each column, compute vector AP: (x_i - 2, y_i - 2, z_i - 5)\n\nThen, compute the cross product AB × AP.\n\nThe cross product AB × AP is:\n\n|i     j     k|\n|36   26   -4|\n|x_i - 2   y_i - 2   z_i - 5|\n\nWhich is:\n\ni*(26*(z_i - 5) - (-4)*(y_i - 2)) - j*(36*(z_i - 5) - (-4)*(x_i - 2)) + k*(36*(y_i - 2) - 26*(x_i - 2))\n\nSo, the cross product is:\n\n[26*(z_i - 5) + 4*(y_i - 2)]i - [36*(z_i - 5) + 4*(x_i - 2)]j + [36*(y_i - 2) - 26*(x_i - 2)]k\n\nThen, the magnitude of this cross product is sqrt[(26*(z_i - 5) + 4*(y_i - 2))^2 + ( - [36*(z_i - 5) + 4*(x_i - 2)])^2 + (36*(y_i - 2) - 26*(x_i - 2))^2]\n\nThe magnitude of AB is sqrt(36^2 + 26^2 + (-4)^2) = sqrt(1296 + 676 + 16) = sqrt(1988). Let me compute that: 1988 is 4*497, so sqrt(4*497) = 2*sqrt(497). Let me compute sqrt(497): 22^2=484, 23^2=529, so sqrt(497)≈22.3, so sqrt(1988)≈2*22.3≈44.6.\n\nSo, |AB|≈44.6.\n\nThen, the distance is |cross product| / |AB|.\n\nSo, for each column, we can compute this distance and see if it's ≤0.5.\n\nBut this seems computationally intensive, but maybe we can find a smarter way.\n\nAlternatively, since the columns are at specific (x, y) positions, perhaps we can compute the minimal distance from the line AB to each column's center (x_i, y_i) and see if it's within 0.5.\n\nWait, but the distance from the line AB to the point (x_i, y_i, z_i) is the same as the distance from the line AB to the point (x_i, y_i, z_i). But since the columns are along the z-axis, the minimal distance from the line AB to the column is the minimal distance from the line AB to the line defined by (x_i, y_i, z) for z between 0 and 6.\n\nBut actually, the minimal distance from the line AB to the column is the minimal distance from the line AB to the line (x_i, y_i, z) for z in [0,6].\n\nBut since the column is a vertical cylinder, the minimal distance from the line AB to the column is the minimal distance from the line AB to the line (x_i, y_i, z) for z in [0,6].\n\nBut since the line (x_i, y_i, z) is just a vertical line, the minimal distance from AB to this line is the distance from AB to (x_i, y_i, 0) and (x_i, y_i, 6). So, the minimal distance is the minimum of the distances from AB to (x_i, y_i, 0) and (x_i, y_i, 6).\n\nTherefore, for each column, we can compute the distance from AB to (x_i, y_i, 0) and (x_i, y_i, 6), and if either of these distances is ≤0.5, then the line AB intersects the column.\n\nSo, let's compute this for each column.\n\nFirst, let's compute the distance from AB to (x_i, y_i, 0):\n\nPoint P1 = (x_i, y_i, 0)\nVector AP1 = (x_i - 2, y_i - 2, -5)\nCross product AB × AP1:\n\n|i     j     k|\n|36   26   -4|\n|x_i - 2   y_i - 2   -5|\n\n= i*(26*(-5) - (-4)*(y_i - 2)) - j*(36*(-5) - (-4)*(x_i - 2)) + k*(36*(y_i - 2) - 26*(x_i - 2))\n\n= i*(-130 + 4*(y_i - 2)) - j*(-180 + 4*(x_i - 2)) + k*(36*(y_i - 2) - 26*(x_i - 2))\n\nSo, the cross product is:\n\n[ -130 + 4*(y_i - 2) ]i + [180 - 4*(x_i - 2) ]j + [36*(y_i - 2) - 26*(x_i - 2)]k\n\nThe magnitude squared is:\n\n[ (-130 + 4y_i - 8)^2 + (180 - 4x_i + 8)^2 + (36y_i - 72 - 26x_i + 52)^2 ]\n\nSimplify:\n\n= [ (-138 + 4y_i)^2 + (188 - 4x_i)^2 + (36y_i - 26x_i - 20)^2 ]\n\nSimilarly, the magnitude of AB is sqrt(1988) as before.\n\nSo, the distance from AB to (x_i, y_i, 0) is sqrt( [ (-138 + 4y_i)^2 + (188 - 4x_i)^2 + (36y_i - 26x_i - 20)^2 ] ) / sqrt(1988)\n\nSimilarly, for point P2 = (x_i, y_i, 6):\n\nVector AP2 = (x_i - 2, y_i - 2, 1)\nCross product AB × AP2:\n\n|i     j     k|\n|36   26   -4|\n|x_i - 2   y_i - 2     1|\n\n= i*(26*1 - (-4)*(y_i - 2)) - j*(36*1 - (-4)*(x_i - 2)) + k*(36*(y_i - 2) - 26*(x_i - 2))\n\n= i*(26 + 4*(y_i - 2)) - j*(36 + 4*(x_i - 2)) + k*(36*(y_i - 2) - 26*(x_i - 2))\n\nSo, the cross product is:\n\n[26 + 4y_i - 8]i + [ -36 - 4x_i + 8 ]j + [36y_i - 72 - 26x_i + 52]k\n\nSimplify:\n\n= [18 + 4y_i]i + [ -28 - 4x_i ]j + [36y_i - 26x_i - 20]k\n\nMagnitude squared:\n\n[ (18 + 4y_i)^2 + (-28 - 4x_i)^2 + (36y_i - 26x_i - 20)^2 ]\n\nSo, the distance from AB to (x_i, y_i, 6) is sqrt( [ (18 + 4y_i)^2 + (-28 - 4x_i)^2 + (36y_i - 26x_i - 20)^2 ] ) / sqrt(1988)\n\nSo, for each column, we can compute these two distances and check if either is ≤0.5.\n\nAlternatively, since sqrt(1988) is a constant, we can compute the squared distances and compare with (0.5)^2 = 0.25.\n\nSo, for each column, compute the squared distance from AB to (x_i, y_i, 0) and (x_i, y_i, 6), and see if either is ≤0.25.\n\nLet me compute this for each column.\n\nFirst, Column 1: (10, 10)\n\nCompute distance to (10,10,0):\n\nCompute numerator squared:\n\n(-138 + 4*10)^2 + (188 - 4*10)^2 + (36*10 - 26*10 - 20)^2\n\n= (-138 + 40)^2 + (188 - 40)^2 + (360 - 260 - 20)^2\n\n= (-98)^2 + (148)^2 + (80)^2\n\n= 9604 + 21904 + 6400\n\n= 9604 + 21904 = 31508; 31508 + 6400 = 37908\n\nSo, squared distance is 37908 / 1988 ≈ 37908 / 1988 ≈ 19.05\n\nWhich is way larger than 0.25, so distance is sqrt(19.05) ≈ 4.36, which is way more than 0.5.\n\nSimilarly, distance to (10,10,6):\n\nCompute numerator squared:\n\n(18 + 4*10)^2 + (-28 - 4*10)^2 + (36*10 - 26*10 - 20)^2\n\n= (18 + 40)^2 + (-28 - 40)^2 + (360 - 260 - 20)^2",
