    "chain_full": "Okay, so I have this problem with two prisms, and I need to figure out at what x-coordinate the second prism will first touch the first one as it slides towards it. Hmm, let me try to visualize this.\n\nFirst, let me understand the setup. Prism 1 is an equilateral triangular prism. Its base is in the xy-plane with vertices at (0, 0, 0), (6, 0, 0), and (3, 3√3, 0). It extends upward to a height of 10 in the z-direction. So, it's a triangular prism with a base that's an equilateral triangle with side length 6, right? Because the distance between (0,0,0) and (6,0,0) is 6, and the distance between (6,0,0) and (3, 3√3, 0) is also 6, and the distance between (3, 3√3, 0) and (0,0,0) is 6 as well. So, it's a regular triangular prism.\n\nPrism 2 is another equilateral triangular prism. Its axis is parallel to the x-axis, which means its triangular face is oriented along the x-axis. One of its triangular faces is centered at (8, 2√3, 5) and it's facing the negative x-direction. So, it's extending in the negative x-direction, which is towards the first prism.\n\nI need to find the x-coordinate where the two prisms first touch when Prism 2 slides towards Prism 1 along the x-axis. So, as Prism 2 moves, its position along the x-axis changes, and we need to find the point where they just touch each other.\n\nLet me think about the dimensions of the prisms. Since both are equilateral triangular prisms, their cross-sections are equilateral triangles with side length 6. The height of each prism is 10 in the z-direction, but since they are prisms, their height is along the z-axis, but their length along the x-axis depends on how they are oriented.\n\nWait, actually, the height of the prism is 10 in the z-direction, but the length along the x-axis would be determined by the projection of the triangular base onto the x-axis. Since the base is an equilateral triangle with side length 6, the projection along the x-axis would be 6 units, right? Because the base is in the