r both be liars. They must be in a state where one is truthful and the other is lying.\n\n**Clara's Statement:**\n\"Alice and I are either both telling the truth or both lying.\" So, Clara is saying that Alice and she are in the same state—both truthful or both lying. In logical terms, this is (A ↔ C), meaning A and C have the same truth value.\n\nAlright, so let me summarize:\n\n- Alice: B → C\n- Ben: (A ⊕ B)\n- Clara: A ↔ C\n\nI need to figure out the truth values of A, B, and C (where A=truthful, B=truthful, C=truthful). Let me consider all possible combinations of A, B, and C being true or false.\n\nThere are 2^3 = 8 possible combinations. Let me list them:\n\n1. A=T, B=T, C=T\n2. A=T, B=T, C=F\n3. A=T, B=F, C=T\n4. A=T, B=F, C=F\n5. A=F, B=T, C=T\n6. A=F, B=T, C=F\n7. A=F, B=F, C=T\n8. A=F, B=F, C=F\n\nNow, I can go through each combination and check if all statements hold.\n\nLet's start with combination 1: A=T, B=T, C=T.\n\n- Alice's statement: B → C. Since B=T and C=T, B → C is true.\n- Ben's statement: (A ⊕ B). Since A=T and B=T, A ⊕ B is false.\n- Clara's statement: A ↔ C. Since A=T and C=T, A ↔ C is true.\n\nSo, in this case, Alice and Clara are telling the truth, Ben is lying. But Ben's statement requires that Alice and he are in exclusive states. However, in this case, both are truthful, which contradicts Ben's statement. So, combination 1 is invalid.\n\nNext, combination 2: A=T, B=T, C=F.\n\n- Alice's statement: B → C. B=T and C=F, so B → C is false.\n- Ben's statement: (A ⊕ B). A=T and B=T, so A ⊕ B is false.\n- Clara's statement: A ↔ C. A=T and C=F, so A ↔ C is false.\n\nSo, in this case, all three are lying. But let's check Ben's statement. Ben says that Alice and he are in exclusive states. If Ben is lying, then Alice and he are either both truthful or both lying. In this case, both are lying, which fits Ben's statement. However, Alice's statement is false, which is okay because Alice is lying. Clara's statement is false, which is also okay because she's lying. So, this combination is possible? Wait, but if all three are lying, does that cause any contradictions? Let me think.\n\nWait, if all three are lying, then Alice is lying, Ben is lying, Clara is lying. Let's check each statement:\n\n- Alice: \"If Ben is telling the truth, then Clara is lying.\" Since Ben is lying, the antecedent is false, so the entire implication is true. But Alice is lying, so her statement must be false. But in reality, it's true, which is a contradiction. So, combination 2 is invalid.\n\nMoving on to combination 3: A=T, B=F, C=T.\n\n- Alice's statement: B → C. B=F, so the implication is true regardless of C. So, Alice is telling the truth.\n- Ben's statement: (A ⊕ B). A=T, B=F, so A ⊕ B is true. But Ben says that Alice and he are in exclusive states. However, in this case, Alice is truthful and Ben is lying, which contradicts Ben's statement. So, combination 3 is invalid.\n\nNext, combination 4: A=T, B=F, C=F.\n\n- Alice's statement: B → C. B=F, so implication is true. Alice is telling the truth.\n- Ben's statement: (A ⊕ B). A=T, B=F, so A ⊕ B is true. Ben says that Alice and he are in exclusive states. But in this case, both are truthful, which contradicts Ben's statement. So, combination 4 is invalid.\n\nCombination 5: A=F, B=T, C=T.\n\n- Alice's statement: B → C. B=T, C=T, so implication is true. Alice is lying.\n- Ben's statement: (A ⊕ B). A=F, B=T, so A ⊕ B is true. Ben says that Alice and he are in exclusive states. But in this case, both are lying, which contradicts Ben's statement. So, combination 5 is invalid.\n\nCombination 6: A=F, B=T, C=F.\n\n- Alice's statement: B → C. B=T, C=F, so implication is false. Alice is lying.\n- Ben's statement: (A ⊕ B). A=F, B=T, so A ⊕ B is true. Ben says that Alice and he are in exclusive states. But in this case, Alice is lying and Ben is truthful, which contradicts Ben's statement. So, combination 6 is invalid.\n\nCombination 7: A=F, B=F, C=T.\n\n- Alice's statement: B → C. B=F, so implication is true. Alice is lying.\n- Ben's statement: (A ⊕ B). A=F, B=F, so A ⊕ B is false. Ben says that Alice and he are in exclusive states. But in this case, both are lying, which contradicts Ben's statement. So, combination 7 is invalid.\n\nCombination 8: A=F, B=F, C=F.\n\n- Alice's statement: B → C. B=F, so implication is true. Alice is lying.\n- Ben's statement: (A ⊕ B). A=F, B=F, so A ⊕ B is false. Ben says that Alice and he are in exclusive states. But in this case, both are lying, which contradicts Ben's statement. So, combination 8 is invalid.\n\nWait a minute, so all combinations except combination 1 and combination 2 lead to contradictions. But combination 1 had Alice and Clara truthful, Ben lying, but that caused a contradiction with Ben's statement. Combination 2 had all three lying, but that also caused a contradiction with Alice's statement.\n\nHmm, so none of the combinations seem to work? That can't be right. Maybe I made a mistake in evaluating the combinations.\n\nLet me double-check combination 1: A=T, B=T, C=T.\n\n- Alice: B → C. T → T is T. So, Alice is truthful.\n- Ben: (A ⊕ B). T ⊕ T is F. So, Ben is lying.\n- Clara: A ↔ C. T ↔ T is T. So, Clara is truthful.\n\nBut Ben is lying, so his statement that Alice and he are in exclusive states must be false. However, Alice and Ben are both truthful, which is not exclusive. So, Ben's statement is false, which is consistent because Ben is lying. So, in this case, Alice and Clara are truthful, Ben is lying. But wait, does this satisfy all statements?\n\nWait, Alice is truthful, Ben is lying, Clara is truthful. So, Alice's statement is true, Ben's statement is false, Clara's statement is true. So, no contradictions here. So, combination 1 is actually consistent.\n\nBut earlier, I thought combination 1 was invalid because I thought Alice's statement was false, but actually, Alice's statement is true because B → C is true when both are true. So, combination 1 is actually a valid solution.\n\nWait, so why did I think combination 1 was invalid earlier? Because I thought Alice's statement was false, but actually, it's true. So, combination 1 is consistent.\n\nSimilarly, let me check combination 2 again: A=T, B=T, C=F.\n\n- Alice: B → C. T → F is F. So, Alice is lying.\n- Ben: (A ⊕ B). T ⊕ T is F. So, Ben is lying.\n- Clara: A ↔ C. T ↔ F is F. So, Clara is lying.\n\nSo, all three are lying. But Ben's statement is that Alice and he are in exclusive states. If all three are lying, then Alice and Ben are both lying, which is consistent with Ben's statement. However, Alice's statement is false, which is consistent because she's lying. But wait, if all three are lying, then Clara is lying, which is consistent with her statement as well. So, is combination 2 a valid solution?\n\nWait, but if all three are lying, then Alice's statement is false, which is okay because she's lying. Ben's statement is false, which is okay because he's lying. Clara's statement is false, which is okay because she's lying. So, combination 2 is also a valid solution.\n\nBut hold on, in combination 2, Ben is lying, so his statement is false. His statement is (A ⊕ B). If A=T and B=T, then (A ⊕ B) is F, which is consistent with him lying. So, combination 2 is actually a valid solution.\n\nBut earlier, I thought combination 2 was invalid because I thought Alice's statement was false, but actually, Alice's statement is true because B → C is true when both are true. So, combination 2 is actually a valid solution.\n\nWait, so both combination 1 and combination 2 are valid? But that can't be, because the problem states that only one person is telling the truth and the others are lying, or maybe it's possible for all to lie? Wait, the problem doesn't specify whether exactly one person is truthful or not. It just says \"who is telling the truth and who is lying.\" So, it's possible that all three are lying, or exactly one is truthful.\n\nBut in combination 1, Alice and Clara are truthful, Ben is lying. So, two people are truthful, which is possible.\n\nIn combination 2, all three are lying, which is also possible.\n\nWait, so does that mean there are two possible solutions? But the problem says \"determine who is telling the truth and who is lying.\" So, maybe both are possible? Or perhaps I made a mistake in evaluating combination 2.\n\nWait, let me check combination 2 again:\n\n- Alice: B → C. B=T, C=F. So, T → F is F. So, Alice is lying.\n- Ben: (A ⊕ B). A=T, B=T. So, T ⊕ T is F. Ben is lying.\n- Clara: A ↔ C. A=T, C=F. So, T ↔ F is F. Clara is lying.\n\nSo, all three are lying. So, that's a valid solution.\n\nBut in combination 1:\n\n- Alice: B → C. T → T is T. Alice is truthful.\n- Ben: (A ⊕ B). T ⊕ T is F. Ben is lying.\n- Clara: A ↔ C. T ↔ T is T. Clara is truthful.\n\nSo, two people are truthful, which is also a valid solution.\n\nWait, so both combination 1 and combination 2 are valid? That seems odd because the problem is asking to determine who is telling the truth and who is lying, implying a unique solution. Maybe I need to check if there are more combinations.\n\nWait, let me check combination 3 again:\n\nA=T, B=F, C=T.\n\n- Alice: B → C. T → T is T. Alice is truthful.\n- Ben: (A ⊕ B). T ⊕ F is T. Ben is lying.\n- Clara: A ↔ C. T ↔ T is T. Clara is truthful.\n\nSo, Alice and Clara are truthful, Ben is lying. But Ben's statement is that Alice and he are in exclusive states. However, in this case, both are truthful, which contradicts Ben's statement. So, combination 3 is invalid.\n\nSimilarly, combination 4: A=T, B=F, C=F.\n\n- Alice: B → C. T → F is F. Alice is lying.\n- Ben: (A ⊕ B). T ⊕ F is T. Ben is lying.\n- Clara: A ↔ C. T ↔ F is F. Clara is lying.\n\nSo, all three are lying. But Ben's statement is that Alice and he are in exclusive states. If all three are lying, then Alice and Ben are both lying, which is consistent with Ben's statement. So, combination 4 is valid.\n\nWait, so combination 4 is also a valid solution. So, now I have three valid solutio